                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "dd1fdcb7-58db-4dc0-89d6-40a56715e3f0",
                        "entry_id": "a0eab93f-27c3-3aca-84e4-f6cbfbbf74a8",
                        "tags": [
                            "comparative"
                        ],
                        "form": "more flagitious"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "063b902c-27c7-47b8-ab08-db89f01e0960",
                        "entry_id": "a0eab93f-27c3-3aca-84e4-f6cbfbbf74a8",
                        "tags": [
                            "superlative"
                        ],
                        "form": "most flagitious"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "61ed709d-e811-38a4-9621-345ce05f8b7e",
                        "entry_id": "a0eab93f-27c3-3aca-84e4-f6cbfbbf74a8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Guilty of terrible crimes; wicked, criminal."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "literary"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ddacbd08-1f29-4c57-bb23-a6d04f48c093",
                                "sense_id": "61ed709d-e811-38a4-9621-345ce05f8b7e",
                                "text": "This young Nobleman was not only a flagitious Punster himself, but was accessary to the Punning of others, by Consent, by Provocation, by Connivance, and by Defence of the Evil committed […] ."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "05256e77-76a3-4365-afc5-6812d59e7484",
                                "sense_id": "61ed709d-e811-38a4-9621-345ce05f8b7e",
                                "text": "The duke found his soldiery half disciplined, flagitious, disorderly and inefficient: he rendered them, in his own words, a \"perfect organ.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "324236f5-c812-42ec-b029-349370070d14",
                                "sense_id": "61ed709d-e811-38a4-9621-345ce05f8b7e",
                                "text": "As flagitious as he can be, Mr. Trump has been a legitimate news figure and, thus, what he had to say should be aired."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7aa49867-728f-35d9-8912-e3af94b2abaf",
                        "entry_id": "a0eab93f-27c3-3aca-84e4-f6cbfbbf74a8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Extremely brutal or wicked; heinous, monstrous."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "literary"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fe65d2ae-4bd4-486a-a6b7-2f0cec9229a1",
                                "sense_id": "7aa49867-728f-35d9-8912-e3af94b2abaf",
                                "text": "As he entered he boomed: \"Monstrous! Flagitious!\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6d52b3ac-bede-40aa-9fd7-96511afb6f82",
                                "sense_id": "7aa49867-728f-35d9-8912-e3af94b2abaf",
                                "text": "But if in Noble Minds ſome Dregs remain, / Not yet purg'd off, of Spleen and ſow'r Diſdain, / Diſcharge that Rage on more Provoking Crimes, / Nor fear a Dearth on theſe Flagitious Times."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d965d50-13be-49de-8f73-430dc5049c32",
                                "sense_id": "7aa49867-728f-35d9-8912-e3af94b2abaf",
                                "text": "Less extensively mischievous, tyrannicide would be less flagitious than populicide; murder of one, though he were a Secretary of State; or—but imagination must stop here—than murder of a promiscuous multitude of unarmed men, women, and children."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
